==============================================================================
A. STELLAR PARALLAX AGAINST A NEARBY DOME (claim a, new decisive test)
==============================================================================
VERIFIED INPUTS (V)
  parallax 768.0665 +/- 0.0499 mas  (Gaia DR3, 2020) = 0.768066 arcsec
  distance 4.2465 ly = 1.3020 pc      d = 4.0176e+13 km
  cross-check 1/d[pc] = 0.768049 arcsec vs quoted 0.768066 -> -0.002% (consistent)
  NOTE: this pass's first draft inverted the article's rounded LEAD (1.3 pc) to get
        0.7692 arcsec and then built a 'the two expressions differ by 0.23%' note.
        That 0.23% was an artefact of the rounding. The parallax is stated directly
        in the body and is used below. 1 AU / 6371 km = 2.35e4.

  The flat-Earth model puts the stars on a nearby inner surface of a 'dome'.
  Scanning every altitude quoted in that literature 1,000-10,000 miles:

  dome altitude      dome R (km)   predicted annual parallax (ALL stars)   excess over measured
     1000 mi            1609        1.917e+10 arcsec        2.50e+10 x
     3000 mi            4828        6.391e+09 arcsec        8.32e+09 x
     5000 mi            8047        3.835e+09 arcsec        4.99e+09 x
     7000 mi           11265        2.739e+09 arcsec        3.57e+09 x
    10000 mi           16093        1.917e+09 arcsec        2.50e+09 x

  Distance the dome would have to sit at to match the measured parallax:
    d = AU/p = 4.0175e+13 km = 1.30 pc = 4.25 ly
    = 8.321e+09 x the 3,000-mile figure  (or 2.496e+09 x the 10,000-mile figure)

  ORDERS OF MAGNITUDE, stated from the table, not asserted:
    smallest excess (10,000 mi dome) = 2.496e+09 = 10^9.4
    largest  excess ( 1,000 mi dome) = 2.496e+10 = 10^10.4
    => the exclusion is ~9 to 10.4 orders of magnitude in angle (NOT 8-10).

  WHY THIS IS DECISIVE, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT DEPEND ON
  * On a single dome every star sits at essentially ONE distance, so ALL of them
    would show the SAME ~1e9-1e10 arcsec annual swing. Measured: 0.768 arcsec for
    the nearest star, a few tenths of an arcsecond for its nearest companions, and
    vastly below 0.01 arcsec for the overwhelming majority of catalogued stars.
    EVERY real parallax is 6 to 10 orders of magnitude below the dome prediction.
    (An earlier draft said '<0.01 arcsec for every other star in the sky', which is
     false: Alpha Centauri ~0.75", Barnard's Star ~0.55", Sirius ~0.38". Softened.)
  * The exclusion is 9-10.4 orders of magnitude in angle (see the computed block
    below): no choice of dome altitude fixes it; 1,000 mi and 10,000 mi both fail
    by the same margin. That robustness is what distinguishes this test from the
  * The only free parameter is the parallax BASELINE. Getting 0.768 arcsec instead
    of ~1e10 arcsec is a direct geometric measurement that Earth moves 1 AU.
    A stationary plane has baseline = 0, hence ZERO annual parallax for every star.
    Measured parallax is therefore positive proof of orbital motion, not merely
    a value inconsistent with the dome. Precisely: the CONVENTIONAL parallax uses
    the 1-AU baseline (a = 1 AU, the parsec definition), while the two six-month-
    apart viewpoints are separated by the orbit's DIAMETER, 2a = 2 AU. An earlier
    draft wrote 'the Earth moves a distance 2a = 1 AU over six months', which is a
    factor-of-2 error and contradicted this section's own a = 1 AU definition.
==============================================================================
B. STELLAR ABERRATION -- an independent measurement of the same orbit
==============================================================================
VERIFIED INPUTS (V): annual aberration maximum '~20 arcseconds', of order v/c,
  caused by Earth's revolution about the Sun  [Wikipedia, 'Aberration (astronomy)']

  mean orbital speed (quoted)    v =  29.780 km/s -> kappa = v/c = 9.9335e-05 rad = 20.489 arcsec
  2*pi*AU/yr (computed here)     v =  29.785 km/s -> kappa = v/c = 9.9353e-05 rad = 20.493 arcsec

  predicted 20.49 arcsec vs observed '~20 arcseconds'  -> agreement to better
  than the 3% implied by that rounding; the classical constant is ~20.5 arcsec.
  NOTE ON THE COMPARISON: the source states the observation in round numbers,
  so this is a 'predicted 20.49 against an observation quoted as about 20'
  agreement, not a 0.1% test. It is nevertheless a two-significant-figure
  prediction of a quantity that a stationary Earth must predict to be EXACTLY zero.

  Aberration is INDEPENDENT of parallax and the two separate cleanly:
    - aberration depends only on observer VELOCITY, not on the star's distance;
    - parallax depends only on baseline and distance.
  Aberration is in phase with the orbit (period 1 year, 90 deg out of phase with
  parallax), so it cannot be an artefact of the same geometry. Either alone
  refutes a stationary plane; together they measure the orbit two ways.

  Third, independent signature of the same velocity -- annual Doppler shift:
    dlambda/lambda = v/c = 9.9335e-05 -> +/- 0.0497 nm at 500 nm (= +/- 0.50 angstrom)
    observed to that precision in stellar spectra (standard astronomical practice).
==============================================================================
B2. STELLAR ANGULAR SIZE AT DOME DISTANCE  (SUPPORTING point, not load-bearing)
==============================================================================
VERIFIED INPUTS (V), Wikipedia, 'Betelgeuse':
  angular diameter 0.047 arcsec (Michelson, uniform disk; 0.055 arcsec corrected
    for limb darkening); radius ~640 R_sun; distance 125 pc (the article's body converts this to 410 ly)

  If that disc were at dome distance:
   at  3000 mi: physical radius = 0.55 m  (diameter 1.10 m)
   at  7000 mi: physical radius = 1.28 m  (diameter 2.57 m)

  CROSS-CHECK that the two verified numbers are mutually consistent:
    implied distance from theta=0.047" and R=640 R_sun:  3.908e+15 km = 413 ly = 127 pc
    implied distance from theta=0.055" and R=640 R_sun:  3.340e+15 km = 353 ly = 108 pc
    article's own distance: 408 ly = 125 pc  -> the 0.047" uniform-disk value
    reproduces it to 1.2%. So the angular diameter and the radius are both real
    measurements of a star hundreds of parsecs away, not a metre-scale lamp.
  PROVENANCE FLAG: the article's own infobox carries parallax 5.95 mas (= 168 pc)
    alongside dist 125 pc -- an internal inconsistency in the source: 168 pc vs
    125 pc is 34.4% on the 125 pc base (the body also carries 410 ly for the 125 pc
    track). It does not bear on this argument, since both values are hundreds of
    parsecs, and 125 pc = 3.86e+15 km vs a 10,000-mile dome (1.61e4 km) is a factor of
    2.4e+11 -- i.e. 10^11.4 to 10^12.4 times further, NOT 10^13.
    An earlier draft wrote '~10^13 times further', which is high by ~1-2 orders.
  LABEL: supporting. Assumes the interferometric angular diameters are real, which
  a dome modeller can deny at some cost in other physics, so it is not the
  load-bearing exclusion. Sections A and B carry that.
